This merge step keeps the first node seen for each `id` (structural first). With the current structural ID scheme (heading/title snake_case), it’s very likely to collide with semantic concept nodes (also snake_case), which will silently drop semantic nodes/metadata. Either ensure structural nodes use a distinct ID namespace (preferred) or change the merge strategy so semantic nodes can override/merge into structural ones when IDs overlap.
```suggestion
def merge_node(existing, incoming):
    merged = dict(existing)
    for k, v in incoming.items():
        if k not in merged:
            merged[k] = v
        elif isinstance(merged[k], dict) and isinstance(v, dict):
            merged[k] = {**merged[k], **v}
        elif isinstance(merged[k], list) and isinstance(v, list):
            merged[k] = merged[k] + [item for item in v if item not in merged[k]]
        elif k != 'id':
            merged[k] = v
    return merged

# Merge: structural nodes first, then semantic (cached + new).
# If ids overlap, merge later semantic data into the existing node
# instead of silently discarding it.
merged_nodes_by_id = {}
merged_node_order = []
for n in structure['nodes'] + cached['nodes'] + new.get('nodes', []):
    node_id = n['id']
    if node_id in merged_nodes_by_id:
        merged_nodes_by_id[node_id] = merge_node(merged_nodes_by_id[node_id], n)
    else:
        merged_nodes_by_id[node_id] = dict(n)
        merged_node_order.append(node_id)

merged_nodes = [merged_nodes_by_id[node_id] for node_id in merged_node_order]
```

